{"title":"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems/Relay 1 Problem A","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Relay_1_Problem_A&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The sum of four consecutive odd integers is 200. What is the largest of these four integers?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Relay_1_Problem_A&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, we can find that the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/2/172bf264a8b2c082be7f24b12296b09faaa2dfff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$390$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/cee3b6e8f7872d6f0908f97be506af681ca6460e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" /> and that the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/8/d282a956275f173977973ea6257778b83c9ac00c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9450$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/f/f9f6006089e736cfeac1045e963191e5dde26f5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 3^{3} \\times 5^{2} \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>From this, we see that the prime factors that are shared between the two numbers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The sum of these factors are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/d/6bd7efed1d291e9eeddb2962b2345b709251fedd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 3 + 5 = \\boxed{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"127\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28without_prime_factorization.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(without_prime_factorization)\">Solution 2 (without prime factorization)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Relay_1_Problem_A&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (without prime factorization)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Without finding the prime factorization of the numbers, we can realize that both numbers are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> since they both end in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This means that the number is divisible by both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We also realize that both numbers are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/b/21b8571a055a897f38b121af824c8f6e37c26d5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 + 9 + 0 = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" />, which is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/ccc09234b45de2febfeab8e5d76fb930468a72c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 + 4 + 5 + 0 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" />, which is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This means that the prime factors are:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>The sum of these factors are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/d/6bd7efed1d291e9eeddb2962b2345b709251fedd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 3 + 5 = \\boxed{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"127\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC\">2018 CEMC CTMC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Relay_0_Problem_C&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems/Relay 0 Problem C (page does not exist)\">Relay 0 Problem C</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Relay_1_Problem_B\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems/Relay 1 Problem B\">Relay 1 Problem B</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Individual_Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems/Individual Problem 1 (page does not exist)\">Individual 1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_CEMC_CTMC_Problems/Individual_Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2018 CEMC CTMC Problems/Individual Problem 2 (page does not exist)\">Individual 2</a> <b>•</b> <a href